      "title": "Sketching the Slope Field of a Differential Equation at Some Isolated Points"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "caaa0c32-756d-5b1d-926c-8bb315d7a38e",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\frac{5}{4}x+(-1)y}{2}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$,$(- \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{3}{2})$,$(- \\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2})}=\\frac{\\frac{5}{4}\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)+(-1)\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{2}=\\frac{\\frac{15}{8}+\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{2}=\\frac{11}{16}, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(- \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{3}{2})}=\\frac{\\frac{5}{4}\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right)+(-1)\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right)}{2}=\\frac{- \\frac{15}{8}+\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}{2}=- \\frac{3}{16}, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(- \\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})}=\\frac{\\frac{5}{4}\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)+(-1)\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}{2}=\\frac{- \\frac{5}{8}+\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right)}{2}=- \\frac{17}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/edb-math/repairs/2026-10-27-topic-audit-2/group-1/staged-assets/17592186172038/hard/1c77a9e70b8af94da9f1e93a4e2b670aa639dc75d76dd54034b0691d703acdb8.png)",
